ENTRY 12471 {"id":12471,"kind":"pi.tool-result","messages":[{"role":"toolResult","text":"     1\timport { spawn } from \"node:child_process\";\n     2\timport { fileURLToPath } from \"node:url\";\n     3\timport { ENV, deriveSandboxToken } from \"@pi-durable-k8s/sandbox-env\";\n     4\t\n     5\t/**\n     6\t * Environment variables the bridge may keep. It needs the in-cluster Kubernetes endpoint (the SDK reads the\n     7\t * claim and Service through the API) and a working PATH/HOME. Everything else in the orchestrator's environment,\n     8\t * notably the database URL, the API tokens and the sandbox master key, stays out of the bridge process. The\n     9\t * sandboxd token is not copied from the parent: bridgeEnv derives it for the bridge's own claim only.\n    10\t */\n    11\tconst ALLOWED = new Set([\n    12\t  \"PATH\",\n    13\t  \"HOME\",\n    14\t  \"TZ\",\n    15\t  \"LANG\",\n    16\t  \"NODE_OPTIONS\",\n    17\t  \"KUBERNETES_SERVICE_HOST\",\n    18\t  \"KUBERNETES_SERVICE_PORT\",\n    19\t  \"KUBERNETES_SERVICE_PORT_HTTPS\",\n    20\t  \"PI_SANDBOX_CONNECTIVITY\",\n    21\t  \"PI_SANDBOX_ENV_BINARY\",\n    22\t]);\n    23\t\n    24\t/** The value of the bridge's `--claim` flag. */\n    25\texport function claimOf(argv: readonly string[]): string {\n    26\t  const claim = argv[argv.indexOf(\"--claim\") + 1];\n    27\t  if (argv.indexOf(\"--claim\") < 0 || claim === undefined || claim === \"\") {\n    28\t    throw new Error(\"bridge-launcher: --claim <name> is required\");\n    29\t  }\n    30\t  return claim;\n    31\t}\n    32\t\n    33\t/**\n    34\t * The bridge's environment: the allow-listed variables plus the sandboxd token of `claimName` only. The master\n    35\t * key is read here and dropped, so a bridge (and anything it spawns) can authenticate to its own sandbox and\n    36\t * to no other. A missing master throws: a bridge without a token could only fail later, less clearly.\n    37\t */\n    38\texport function bridgeEnv(\n    39\t  env: Readonly<Record<string, string | undefined>>,\n    40\t  claimName: string,\n    41\t): Record<string, string> {\n    42\t  const master = env[ENV.masterKey];\n    43\t  if (master === undefined || master === \"\") {\n    44\t    throw new Error(`bridge-launcher: ${ENV.masterKey} is not set`);\n    45\t  }\n    46\t  const kept: Record<string, string> = {};\n    47\t  for (const [name, value] of Object.entries(env)) {\n    48\t    if (value !== undefined && ALLOWED.has(name)) kept[name] = value;\n    49\t  }\n    50\t  kept[ENV.authToken] = deriveSandboxToken(master, claimName);\n    51\t  return kept;\n    52\t}\n    53\t\n    54\t/**\n    55\t * The program pi-env's Connection spawns for a session. It starts the real bridge (sandbox-env's cli.js) with the\n    56\t * trimmed environment and passes stdio straight through, so the pi-env protocol is untouched. When pi-env\n    57\t * ends the connection (SIGTERM or closed stdin) the bridge ends too.\n    58\t */\n    59\tfunction main(): void {\n    60\t  const cli = fileURLToPath(import.meta.resolve(\"@pi-durable-k8s/sandbox-env\")).replace(\n    61\t    /index\\.js$/,\n    62\t    \"cli.js\",\n    63\t  );\n    64\t  const args = process.argv.slice(2);\n    65\t  let env: Record<string, string>;\n    66\t  try {\n    67\t    env = bridgeEnv(process.env, claimOf(args));\n    68\t  } catch (error) {\n    69\t    process.stderr.write(`${error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error)}\\n`);\n    70\t    process.exit(2);\n    71\t  }\n    72\t  const child = spawn(process.execPath, [cli, ...args], {\n    73\t    env,\n    74\t    stdio: \"inherit\",\n    75\t  });\n    76\t  for (const signal of [\"SIGTERM\", \"SIGINT\", \"SIGHUP\"] as const) {\n    77\t    process.on(signal, () => child.kill(signal));\n    78\t  }\n    79\t  child.on(\"exit\", (code, signal) => {\n    80\t    process.exit(code ?? (signal === null ? 1 : 128));\n    81\t  });\n    82\t}\n    83\t\n    84\t// Only run when started as the launcher, not when a test imports bridgeEnv.\n    85\tif (\n    86\t  process.argv[1] !== undefined &&\n    87\t  import.meta.url === new URL(`file://${process.argv[1]}`).href\n    88\t) {\n    89\t  main();\n    90\t}\ndeploy/orchestrator.yaml:35:          workingDir: /code/app\ndeploy/ingest.yaml:61:          workingDir: /app/code\npackages/orchestrator/src/bridge-launcher.ts:16:  \"NODE_OPTIONS\",\npackages/orchestrator/src/bridge-launcher.ts:20:  \"PI_SANDBOX_CONNECTIVITY\",\npackages/sandbox-env/src/config.ts:12:  connectivity: \"PI_SANDBOX_CONNECTIVITY\",\npackages/sandbox-env/test/config.test.ts:43:        PI_SANDBOX_CONNECTIVITY: \"x\",\npackages/sandbox-env/test/conformance.e2e.test.ts:3://   PI_SANDBOX_CONNECTIVITY (default in-cluster-service).\npackages/vendor/agent-sandbox-client/src/types.ts:20:   * port-forward. Defaults to `loadFromDefault()` (KUBECONFIG, then\npackages/vendor/agent-sandbox-client/src/sandbox-client.ts:412:        \"Set KUBECONFIG, provide ~/.kube/config, or run inside a cluster.\",\npackages/vendor/agent-sandbox-client/README.md:182:By default the client loads `KUBECONFIG`, then `~/.kube/config`, then the in-cluster\n     1\t// The only module allowed to import the vendored Agent Sandbox SDK (see packages/vendor).\n     2\timport { SandboxClient } from \"agentic-sandbox-client\";\n     3\timport type { RemoteProcess, StartProcess } from \"./bridge.js\";\n     4\t\n     5\texport type Connectivity = \"in-cluster-service\" | \"in-cluster-pod-ip\" | \"port-forward\";\n     6\t\n     7\texport interface SandboxTarget {\n     8\t  claimName: string;\n     9\t  namespace: string;\n    10\t  connectivity: Connectivity;\n    11\t  /** Bearer token for sandboxd; plaintext on the wire with the in-cluster modes. */\n    12\t  authToken?: string;\n    13\t}\n    14\t\n    15\t/** Looks the SandboxClaim up on first use, so constructing this does no I/O. */\n    16\texport function sandboxStart(target: SandboxTarget): StartProcess {\n    17\t  return async (command, args, handlers) => {\n    18\t    const client = new SandboxClient({\n    19\t      namespace: target.namespace,\n    20\t      sandboxd: {\n    21\t        connectivity: target.connectivity,\n    22\t        ...(target.authToken === undefined ? {} : { authToken: target.authToken }),\n    23\t      },\n    24\t    });\n    25\t    // Release the handle with closeLocal(): Sandbox.close() also DELETES the SandboxClaim, which\n    26\t    // would destroy the session's sandbox (and its PVC) every time a bridge exits.\n    27\t    const sandbox = await client.getSandbox(target.claimName, target.namespace);\n    28\t    try {\n    29\t      // No pty: a terminal would merge stderr into stdout and mangle binary bytes.\n    30\t      const handle = await sandbox.commands.start(command, args, {\n    31\t        onStdout: (chunk) => {\n    32\t          handlers.onStdout(chunk);\n    33\t        },\n    34\t        onStderr: (chunk) => {\n    35\t          handlers.onStderr(chunk);\n    36\t        },\n    37\t      });\n    38\t      const process: RemoteProcess = {\n    39\t        write: (data) => handle.write(data),\n    40\t        closeStdin: () => handle.closeStdin(),\n    41\t        wait: () => handle.wait(),\n    42\t        close: async () => {\n    43\t          await handle.close();\n    44\t          await sandbox.closeLocal();\n    45\t        },\n    46\t      };\n    47\t      return process;\n    48\t    } catch (error) {\n    49\t      await sandbox.closeLocal();\n    50\t      throw error;\n    51\t    }\n    52\t  };\n    53\t}\n    54\t\n    55\texport interface ClaimRequest extends SandboxTarget {\n    56\t  /** SandboxWarmPool the claim references; a pool with `replicas: 0` is a plain template alias. */\n    57\t  warmPool: string;\n    58\t}\n    59\t\n    60\t/**\n    61\t * Creates the named SandboxClaim, or adopts it when it exists (create-or-attach, so a restarted\n    62\t * orchestrator calls it again for every session), and waits until its Sandbox is ready. The handle is\n    63\t * released with closeLocal(): only session deletion may delete a claim, and this module has no delete.\n    64\t */\n    65\texport async function ensureClaim(request: ClaimRequest): Promise<void> {\n    66\t  const client = new SandboxClient({\n    67\t    namespace: request.namespace,\n    68\t    sandboxd: {\n    69\t      connectivity: request.connectivity,\n    70\t      ...(request.authToken === undefined ? {} : { authToken: request.authToken }),\n    71\t    },\n    72\t  });\n    73\t  const sandbox = await client.createSandbox(request.warmPool, request.namespace, {\n    74\t    claimName: request.claimName,\n    75\t    adoptExisting: true,\n    76\t  });\n    77\t  await sandbox.closeLocal();\n    78\t}\n","isError":false}]}
ENTRY 12483 {"id":12483,"kind":"pi.assistant","messages":[{"role":"assistant","text":"[thinking]","calls":["bash({\"command\":\"cd /workspace/repo && grep -R -n -i -E 'kubeconfig|run.*orchestrator|local.*orchestrator' README.md docs packages/orchestrator | head -100; cat -n deploy/orchestrator.yaml | head -90; sed -n '140,160p' docs/session-api.md\"})"]}]}
ENTRY 12485 {"id":12485,"kind":"pi.tool-result","messages":[{"role":"toolResult","text":"README.md:31:- Sandboxes run under gVisor. Network policies (written for Cilium) block sandboxes from the orchestrator, the\ndocs/session-api.md:64:- **No double durability, no re-run.** Pi Durable owns run recovery. The orchestrator never resubmits a row that is\ndocs/session-api.md:65:  `submitted`, and never re-runs anything because a run died. If the orchestrator dies after Pi Durable accepted a\ndocs/session-api.md:167:- **Interrupted commands.** A command that was aborted (cancel, steer), timed out, or was running when the orchestrator\ndocs/decisions/0003-recycle-pod-on-interrupt.md:23:**In the running orchestrator.** `Sandboxes.lazyEnv` classifies each `exec` outcome. When the last call in flight of\ndocs/research/phase1-apis.md:188:  new SandboxClient({ kubeConfig?, namespace?, sandboxReadyTimeout?, sandboxd?: { connectivity?, authToken?, routerNamespace?, ... }, logger?, quiet?, enableTracing? })\ndocs/research/phase1-apis.md:212:- `examples/pi-code-agent/` (`Dockerfile`, `sandbox.yaml`, `README.md`, `run-test-kind.sh`; docs mirror `site/content/docs/use-cases/examples/pi-code-agent/_index.md`). It runs the **interactive Pi coding-agent TUI inside a Sandbox** (`node:24-bookworm-slim`, `npm i -g @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent@0.84.1`, `kubectl attach -it`, plain `Sandbox` CRD, two PVCs, API key via Secret). It does NOT use sandboxd, pi-env, SandboxClaim, Pi Durable or an outside orchestrator, so it is \"Pi inside\" rather than \"orchestrator outside, tools inside\". Good place to put the Phase 5 example next to it.\ndocs/research/phase1-apis.md:242:- gVisor `runtimeClassName` is only a commented-out hint in upstream examples; nothing upstream confirms the in-cluster-service + gVisor path beyond README statements. NetworkPolicy must admit the orchestrator on 8080 and 9090.\ndocs/network-policy.md:20:| orchestrator (also `e2e-runner`, `pi-orchestrator-b`) | `app.kubernetes.io/name=pi-orchestrator` | `deploy/orchestrator.yaml`, `tests/e2e/runner-pod.yaml` |\npackages/orchestrator/src/kube.ts:4:  KubeConfig,\npackages/orchestrator/src/kube.ts:70:  const kc = new KubeConfig();\npackages/orchestrator/src/kube.ts:128:  const kc = new KubeConfig();\npackages/orchestrator/src/kube.ts:172:  const kc = new KubeConfig();\n     1\t# One replica, Recreate: the Postgres adapter allows a single owner per schema.\n     2\t# Code comes from the orchestrator-code PVC (deploy/push-code.sh), so no image is pushed.\n     3\t# The DB URI comes from the CNPG-generated secret pi-pg-app and is never printed.\n     4\tapiVersion: apps/v1\n     5\tkind: Deployment\n     6\tmetadata:\n     7\t  name: pi-orchestrator\n     8\t  labels:\n     9\t    app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-orchestrator\n    10\tspec:\n    11\t  replicas: 1\n    12\t  strategy:\n    13\t    type: Recreate\n    14\t  selector:\n    15\t    matchLabels:\n    16\t      app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-orchestrator\n    17\t      app.kubernetes.io/instance: pi-orchestrator\n    18\t  template:\n    19\t    metadata:\n    20\t      labels:\n    21\t        app.kubernetes.io/name: pi-orchestrator\n    22\t        app.kubernetes.io/instance: pi-orchestrator\n    23\t    spec:\n    24\t      serviceAccountName: pi-orchestrator\n    25\t      securityContext:\n    26\t        runAsNonRoot: true\n    27\t        runAsUser: 1000\n    28\t        runAsGroup: 1000\n    29\t        fsGroup: 1000\n    30\t        seccompProfile:\n    31\t          type: RuntimeDefault\n    32\t      containers:\n    33\t        - name: orchestrator\n    34\t          image: node:22-bookworm-slim\n    35\t          workingDir: /code/app\n    36\t          command: [node, dist/main.js]\n    37\t          # PI_MODEL_PROVIDER, PI_MODEL_ID, PI_MODEL_ALLOWLIST, PI_SESSION_TURN_BUDGET.\n    38\t          envFrom:\n    39\t            - configMapRef: { name: pi-model-config }\n    40\t          env:\n    41\t            - { name: HOME, value: /tmp }\n    42\t            - { name: PI_ORCH_NAMESPACE, value: pi-durable-poc }\n    43\t            - { name: PI_ORCH_WARMPOOL, value: pi-env-pool }\n    44\t            - { name: PI_ORCH_PORT, value: \"8081\" }\n    45\t            - { name: PI_DURABLE_SCHEMA, value: durable }\n    46\t            - name: POD_NAME\n    47\t              valueFrom: { fieldRef: { fieldPath: metadata.name } }\n    48\t            - name: PI_DURABLE_DATABASE_URL\n    49\t              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: pi-pg-app, key: uri } }\n    50\t            # Master key of the per-sandbox sandboxd tokens: HMAC(master, claim). Never reaches a bridge or a sandbox.\n    51\t            - name: PI_SANDBOX_MASTER_KEY\n    52\t              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: sandboxd-auth, key: master } }\n    53\t            # Same bearer tokens as ingest (create with deploy/gen-api-secret.sh): the stream endpoint\n    54\t            # authenticates the caller again and checks the session owner.\n    55\t            - name: PI_API_TOKENS\n    56\t              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: api-auth, key: tokens } }\n    57\t            # Model calls go through the OneCLI gateway with this agent token (an ExternalSecret from secret-store,\n    58\t            # deploy/onecli-externalsecret.yaml). Optional so the scripted model runs without it. It is read from the\n    59\t            # environment once and never logged; bridge-launcher strips it from every bridge child.\n    60\t            - name: ONECLI_AGENT_TOKEN\n    61\t              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: pi-onecli, key: agent-token, optional: true } }\n    62\t            # Phase 4c: management API key for per-session OneCLI agents (repo sessions only; optional).\n    63\t            - name: ONECLI_API_KEY\n    64\t              valueFrom: { secretKeyRef: { name: pi-onecli-admin, key: api-key, optional: true } }\n    65\t            - { name: PI_ONECLI_API, value: \"http://onecli.onecli:10256\" }\n    66\t            - { name: PI_ONECLI_GATEWAY, value: \"http://onecli.onecli:10255\" }\n    67\t            - { name: PI_ONECLI_CA_FILE, value: /etc/onecli/ca.pem }\n    68\t            # No process-wide HTTPS_PROXY: only pi-ai's model calls use a dispatcher scoped to the gateway. NO_PROXY\n    69\t            # is the belt and braces list (cluster, Kubernetes API, Postgres) the orchestrator also applies itself.\n    70\t            - { name: NO_PROXY, value: \"localhost,127.0.0.1,.svc,.cluster.local,kubernetes.default,pi-pg-rw,10.0.0.0/8\" }\n    71\t            # Test-only, both off unless set. CONTROL mounts the authenticated Phase 3 scripted-session surface\n    72\t            # under /control. TEST_FAULTS honours payload.testFault on inbox rows, which only SQL can write\n    73\t            # (ingest never does), so the Phase 4 suite can kill the orchestrator between submit and mark.\n    74\t          ports:\n    75\t            - { name: control, containerPort: 8081 }\n    76\t          readinessProbe:\n    77\t            httpGet: { path: /readyz, port: control }\n    78\t            periodSeconds: 3\n    79\t          resources:\n    80\t            requests: { cpu: 100m, memory: 256Mi }\n    81\t            limits: { cpu: \"1\", memory: 2Gi }\n    82\t          securityContext:\n    83\t            allowPrivilegeEscalation: false\n    84\t            capabilities:\n    85\t              drop: [ALL]\n    86\t          volumeMounts:\n    87\t            - { name: code, mountPath: /code, readOnly: true }\n    88\t            - { name: onecli-ca, mountPath: /etc/onecli, readOnly: true }\n    89\t            - { name: github-app, mountPath: /etc/pi-github-app, readOnly: true }\n    90\t      volumes:\n  stream stay readable, and `DELETE` works. The status commits before the conversation is aborted, so the abort is idempotent and repeated: by a retried failure, by any input that finds the session `failed`, and at orchestrator start for sessions failed in the last 24 h. Other model errors (rate limits, network) do not fail the session.\n\n## Sessions and sandboxes\n\n- Session ids are random UUIDs (v4), never sequential. The owner is stored per session as `owner` (the token\n  entry's name) and `owner_subject` (the stable identity: `static:<owner>` for static tokens, issuer + subject for\n  OIDC later); the owner check compares the subject.\n- Every SandboxClaim carries the label `pi-durable-k8s/session-id: <session UUID>`. The orchestrator creates the\n  claim with that label, and adopts an existing claim of the same name only if the label matches. A claim with the\n  right name and another or no label is refused, and never deleted by session deletion. (Claims that predate this\n  label are therefore not adopted; the claim name `pi-conv-<conversation id>` alone proves nothing.)\n- The bridge processes (one Node process per session with a sandbox) are started through\n  `bridge-launcher.js`, which gives them only `PI_SANDBOX_AUTH_TOKEN` (the token of their own claim, derived from the\n  master key; see `docs/decisions/0001-per-sandbox-sandboxd-token.md`), the in-cluster Kubernetes endpoint, `PATH`\n  and `HOME`. The database URL, the API tokens, the OneCLI agent token and the sandbox master key are not in their\n  environment (checked by the e2e suite in `/proc/<pid>/environ`).\n- An existing claim is adopted only if its `SANDBOXD_AUTH_TOKEN_SHA256` env matches the token hash the orchestrator\n  derives for it. A claim from before per-sandbox tokens (or after a master rotation) is refused with an error that\n  says to delete the claim and its PVC.\n- Every Pi Durable submission carries `requestId = <inbox row id>`: `SubmissionDraft.requestId` is part of the\n  1.5.2 type of `Conversation.submit()`, and its README documents that a repeated `requestId` returns the existing\n","isError":false}]}
ENTRY 12493 {"id":12493,"kind":"pi.assistant","messages":[{"role":"assistant","text":"[thinking]## Findings\n\n### 1. Bridge can read the launcher’s master key through `/proc`\n\n- Severity: high\n- Confidence: high\n- Location: packages/orchestrator/src/bridge-launcher.ts:72\n- What happens: The launcher retains the orchestrator’s full environment while its bridge child runs. In the deployed Linux container, both processes run as the same user, so a compromised bridge can read `/proc/<parent-pid>/environ` and recover the sandbox master key and other credentials. Filtering the child’s environment therefore does not enforce the intended per-claim credential isolation.\n- Evidence: `spawn(process.execPath, [cli, ...args], { env, stdio: \"inherit\" })`\n- Fix idea: Put the bridge in a separate security boundary that cannot inspect the launcher or orchestrator processes, such as a separate pod or appropriately isolated UID and PID namespace.\n\n### 2. Launcher silently does nothing when its path contains URL-special characters\n\n- Severity: low\n- Confidence: high\n- Location: packages/orchestrator/src/bridge-launcher.ts:87\n- What happens: If the repository or installation path contains `#` or `?`, constructing a file URL by string interpolation treats part of the path as a fragment or query. The comparison fails, `main()` is not called, and sandbox connections exit without starting the bridge.\n- Evidence: `import.meta.url === new URL(\\`file://${process.argv[1]}\\`).href`\n- Fix idea: Convert the filesystem path with `pathToFileURL()` before comparing it with `import.meta.url`.\n\nEND-OF-REPORT"}]}
DONE
